{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-149467",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the number of the chosen answer. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{0,1,…,6\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{0,1,…,6\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{0+6}{2}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen answer is $3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
